1
00:00:00,265 --> 00:00:06,145
The CLI and API in this synthetic order
service both validate quantity,

2
00:00:06,428 --> 00:00:07,951
and the rule is duplicated.

3
00:00:08,335 --> 00:00:12,950
Issue thirty-one asks us to share the
validator and keep the behavior of both

4
00:00:12,950 --> 00:00:13,600
callers.

5
00:00:13,959 --> 00:00:18,532
Open the issue and point to the expected
outputs before asking for edits.

6
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:23,351
We want a small structural change with
the same results for the same inputs.

7
00:00:24,893 --> 00:00:29,786
Run the existing suite first. The
synthetic baseline shows eight passing

8
00:00:29,786 --> 00:00:34,160
tests, including normal and boundary
cases around the two callers.

9
00:00:34,645 --> 00:00:38,039
Capture the starting commit and command
with that result.

10
00:00:38,659 --> 00:00:41,534
This gives us a comparison point after
each stage.

11
00:00:41,985 --> 00:00:46,284
If a boundary isn't already covered,
agree on how to capture it before you

12
00:00:46,319 --> 00:00:47,977
ask the agent to move the rule.

13
00:00:49,768 --> 00:00:54,477
Ask the agent to map the two callers and
propose one stage for each migration.

14
00:00:54,989 --> 00:00:59,530
The first stage extracts the rule and
points the CLI at it.

15
00:00:59,824 --> 00:01:04,679
The second stage changes the API. Check
that the agent can name the files and

16
00:01:04,679 --> 00:01:05,871
validation command.

17
00:01:06,254 --> 00:01:10,893
Then approve the first stage and leave
the second stage waiting for the diff

18
00:01:10,928 --> 00:01:11,932
and test evidence.

19
00:01:13,807 --> 00:01:17,656
Look at Git status first so a newly
created file is visible.

20
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:24,029
In the example, the shared validator and
the CLI are the only files changed.

21
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:26,972
Open the validator and check the
comparison,

22
00:01:27,299 --> 00:01:30,085
then follow the call in the CLI.

23
00:01:30,778 --> 00:01:34,671
The tests should still show the same
behavior for quantity one, zero,

24
00:01:34,795 --> 00:01:36,072
and negative values.

25
00:01:36,523 --> 00:01:38,957
Review that evidence before the API
stage.

26
00:01:40,723 --> 00:01:44,819
Here's the staged handoff. The agent
proposes opening the API and a

27
00:01:44,819 --> 00:01:45,828
deployment file.

28
00:01:46,144 --> 00:01:51,337
Pause on the deployment entry. The issue
names an API caller and a behavior

29
00:01:51,337 --> 00:01:55,560
check, so there is no reason to approve
that file in this step.

30
00:01:56,067 --> 00:02:00,499
Tell the agent which call site belongs
to the scope and ask for a revised plan

31
00:02:00,781 --> 00:02:02,754
before it edits anything further.

32
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:09,275
After the API stage, confirm the names
of all three changed files and inspect

33
00:02:09,275 --> 00:02:10,014
the new call.

34
00:02:10,419 --> 00:02:13,736
Run the same test command again, on the
final state.

35
00:02:14,244 --> 00:02:19,021
Here all eight tests still pass. Compare
the observed boundary cases with the

36
00:02:19,021 --> 00:02:21,363
baseline and check the public outputs.

37
00:02:21,768 --> 00:02:24,580
The final run is the one that supports
this exact diff.

38
00:02:26,348 --> 00:02:30,304
The pull request can now explain what
changed, what stayed compatible,

39
00:02:30,563 --> 00:02:31,633
and how we checked it.

40
00:02:32,017 --> 00:02:35,698
Include both runs and the scope decision
about the deployment file.

41
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:41,152
A separate human reviewer can inspect
the diff and check status using GitHub

42
00:02:41,163 --> 00:02:44,313
CLI from the authorized terminal.

43
00:02:44,658 --> 00:02:48,810
They can follow the same evidence trail
without having to watch your agent

44
00:02:48,810 --> 00:02:49,460
session.

45
00:02:50,765 --> 00:02:54,861
For practice, find a second repeated
rule in the training checkout.

46
00:02:55,177 --> 00:02:59,070
Decide what the first stage proves and
what the second stage proves.

47
00:02:59,454 --> 00:03:02,649
Keep both stages small enough to explain
in a sentence.

48
00:03:03,202 --> 00:03:07,294
If you can point to a baseline, a stop
point, and a final independent check,

49
00:03:07,554 --> 00:03:10,181
you have a refactoring workflow you can
reuse.
